/// Chapter 27 of the guide, written from md-docs/27-content.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page ContentGuide(path: "/docs/guide/content", title: "Content", description: "The Markdown element, .md pages with front matter, search and sharing tags, canonical links, sitemaps, JSON-LD and code blocks.", layout: DocsShell("Content", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "27" }), blurb: "Markdown, pages written as .md files, code on the page, and what the build writes for search engines and link previews.", source: "md-docs/27-content.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "the-markdown-element", label: "The Markdown element"), Anchor(id: "md-pages", label: ".md pages"), Anchor(id: "search-and-sharing", label: "Search and sharing"), Anchor(id: "a-checklist-for-being-found", label: "A checklist for being found"), Anchor(id: "code-on-the-page", label: "Code on the page")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/static-and-spa", prevLabel: "26. {t("ch.26")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/media", nextLabel: "28. {t("ch.28")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("A site is also its words: pages of prose, a blog, docs. This chapter covers writing content in Markdown, showing code, and what the build writes for search engines and link previews. Pre-rendering is [Static or single-page](/docs/guide/static-and-spa); pictures and video are [Media](/docs/guide/media).")

        Heading("The Markdown element", id: "the-markdown-element").h2

        Markdown("`Markdown(text)` renders a small, predictable Markdown — the same at build time and in the browser, so a hydrated page repaints what was painted:")

        CodeBlock(##"page Notes(path: "/") {
    state body = "# Release notes\n\nVersion **3.3** adds:\n\n- Markdown pages\n- `wf docs`\n\n> Ship it."
    Markdown(body)
    Markdown("Inline *emphasis*, a [link](/about) and an ![image](/img.png).")
}"##, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Blocks: `#`–`######` headings, paragraphs, fenced code with a language (`` ```js ``), `>` quotes, `-`/`*` and `1.` lists (one level), `---` rules. Inline: `` `code` ``, `**strong**`, `*em*`/`_em_`, links, images. Raw HTML in the text is shown as text, not run — Markdown from a user or an API is safe to render.")

        Heading(".md pages", id: "md-pages").h2

        Markdown("A Markdown file in `src/` is a page. Its front matter names the page's attributes; the rest is the body:")

        CodeBlock(#"---
path: /guide/install
title: Installing
description: Get wf on your machine in a minute.
layout: DocsShell
type: article
---

# Installing

Download a release, or build from source:

```sh
cargo install webfluent
```

Then `wf init my-site`."#, title: "markdown")

        Markdown("The front matter keys are the page's attributes: `path` (default: `/` for `index.md`, else `/<file-name>`), `title`, `description`, `layout`, `image`, `type`, `noindex`. The page's name is the file's stem, capitalised.\n\n`layout: DocsShell` frames the body in a component with a default slot — the same one a `.wf` page names ([chapter 6](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing#layouts)):")

        CodeBlock(#"component DocsShell {
    slot
    Row(gap: .lg) {
        Sidebar {
            Sidebar.Item(to: "/guide/install") { Text("Installing") }
            Sidebar.Item(to: "/guide/pages") { Text("Pages") }
        }
        Container { children }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A docs site is a folder of `.md` files, one layout, and a `.wf` for the `app`. (`wf docs` is something else: a gallery of every component the project can use — [chapter 37](/docs/guide/cli#wf-docs-wf-registry-wf-types).)")

        Heading("Search and sharing", id: "search-and-sharing").h2

        Markdown("For every page the build writes the standard head from the page's attributes and `meta.*` in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Acme",
    "description": "Tools for small teams.",
    "lang": "en",
    "site_url": "https://acme.example",
    "site_name": "Acme Inc.",
    "image": "/og-default.png",
    "favicon": "/favicon.svg",
    "sitemap": true,
    "fonts": ["https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter&display=swap"],
    "stylesheets": ["/print.css"]
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("- `<title>` and `<meta name=\"description\">` from the page's `title:` and `description:` — the snippet a search result shows and a link preview quotes. Keep the description under ~150 characters.\n- A canonical link and `og:url`, when `site_url` is set (a relative canonical is worse than none, so without `site_url` the build emits none).\n- Open Graph and Twitter card tags: `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:image` (the page's `image:` or the site's), `og:type` from `type:` (`website` or `article`), `og:site_name`.\n- JSON-LD: an `Organization`, a `WebPage` or `Article`, and a `BreadcrumbList` derived from the route's segments.\n- `hreflang` alternates for each locale when the project has several.\n- `<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex\">` and no sitemap entry for a `noindex: true` page.\n\nA page adds its own tags with `head \{ \}` ([chapter 6](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing#per-page-head-tags)).")

        CodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, summary: String, cover: String, date: String, body: String }

data posts: [Post] = "posts.json"

page PostPage(path: "/posts/:slug", slug: String, type: "article", paths: posts.map(p => p.slug)) {
    derived post = posts.find(p => p.slug == slug)
    head {
        meta(property: "og:title", content: post?.title ?? "Post")
        meta(property: "og:image", content: post?.cover ?? "/og-default.png")
        meta(property: "article:published_time", content: post?.date ?? "")
    }
    if let p = post {
        Image(src: p.cover, alt: p.title)
        Heading(p.title).h1
        Text(format(p.date, .date, "long")).muted
        Markdown(p.body)
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The `title:` and `description:` of a `:param` page may name its parameters — `title: \"\{slug\} — Blog\"` — and each pre-rendered file, and the live page's tab, has that route's value. The title may also splice an expression over the parameters and the program's constants and data — `title: \"\{posts.find(p => p.slug == slug)?.title ?? slug\} — Blog\"` — which the build works out for each file and the router again on each visit. Anything else the head should say about the entry goes in `head \{ \}`, as above; the runtime keeps those tags current as the parameter changes.")

        Heading("A checklist for being found", id: "a-checklist-for-being-found").h2

        Markdown("- Set `meta.site_url` — without it there are no canonical links, no sitemap and no absolute sharing URLs.\n- Give every page a `title:` and a `description:` of about 150 characters (`S01`–`S03` warn when they are missing or too long).\n- Pre-render: `build.ssg: true`, so the text is in the HTML.\n- One `h1` per page, headings in order (`A11`, `A12`).\n- An `image:` per page that is shared, or `meta.image` for the site — 1200 × 630 pixels suits most previews.\n- `noindex: true` on pages that should not be found: sign-in, thanks, drafts.\n- Check the result: view a built page's source, and paste a URL into a link-preview debugger.")

        Heading("Code on the page", id: "code-on-the-page").h2

        Markdown("`Code(\"…\")` is inline code; `Code(\"…\").block` a block. `language:` colours it — `wf`, `json`, `bash` or `css` — at build time and in the browser alike, with the theme's `syntax-*` tokens; a `Markdown` fence with one of those languages is coloured the same way.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Snippets(path: "/") {
    Text("Run ")  Code("wf serve")  Text(" to start.")
    Code("page Home(path: \"/\") {\n    Text(\"Hi\")\n}", language: "wf").block
    Code("$ wf build", language: "bash").block
}"#, title: "wf")
    }
}
